Date: Fri, 12 Nov 1993 13:51:29 -0500 (EST)
From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
Subject: Response to Nan Wolfslayer

First, my apologies to this list for my apparent double-posting of the message
to which Nan refers.  I posted the message once, but some problems with my e-mail
address caused the message to be repeated.

Second, my congratulations to Nan for the coolest surname in recent memory.
But on to your questions:

> I think its a great idea to hold social science research to some criterion 
> of real-world usefulness, however

> a)  what do you mean by *some* criterion?  Whose criterion is it? (those
> feisty little critters...)

As in any free market system, the only criterion is that someone be *willing* 
to pay for the research.

> b)  how on earth (pun intended) would you define what *real-world* 
> usefulness is?  Yawza!  My brain spins at the thought of it.  Can you
> (general we) define a product before it is fully conceived and introduced
> by an x market value?  How could such a value be set?  What is our
> defination of value, anyway?  Is it a product which produces direct
> financial gain?

See my answer to (a).  Usefulness is usefulness to some group or individual who 
is willing to pay.  Somone always has to make a decision about whether an idea 
will pay off.  In my opinion, the "problems" you mention occur mostly because of 
the assumption that coercive force (esp. taxation) must be used to obtain 
funding, and hence decide what's useful.

> c)  what do you mean by synthetic and analytical?  Do you define these terms
> in a Kantian way, or by some other means?

I haven't read Kant.  By "analytical" I mean the approach that looks at a complex 
system and posits abstract units (phoneme, word, mind, spirit, culture, society) 
to account for the observed behavior.  Such systems inevitably run into deep 
problems, because complex systems are not based on the mathematical notions (set 
membership, hierarchy) on which the abstract analyses are based.  (I exclude 
domains like particle physics, which are not complex systems in the sense that 
biological systems are.)  Picking a particular analysis inevitably leaves you 
with  (1) lots of exceptions to your rules, (2) the possibility of alternative 
analyses that capture a lot of generalizations about the data that your analysis 
fails to capture.

By *synthetic* I mean a "bottom-up" approach that avoids positing abstract units, 
and sticks instead to physically observable quantities, like energy flow, trying 
to model how the physics could account for the same behavior that the analytical 
approach tries to handle, without all the ontological baggage.  This has been a 
paradigm in connectionist modelling for years, and, though I disagree with the 
methodology and arguments of many connectionist researchers, I consider the 
enterprise itself to be more promising than the traditional symbol-based 
alternatives. 

> All questions aside, your idea is interesting but I don't really see how 
> something synthesized by human beings and left to evolve will say
> anything at all about our wourld in general - or particular.  I can see 
> where it might say something interesting about what is being synthesized,
> or that it might say something else about the expectations and psychological
> conditions of the synthesizer but - how could we then expand that 
> particular fractionation into a general theory of behavior?

To me, the only really interesting question is: "How can things like life and 
language have developed from inanimate matter?"  Coming up with more an more 
theories about what life and language are "really like" isn't going to answer 
that question.  Trying to *model* how life and language could have arisen looks 
like a more promising alternative.  People who disagree with a model (or an 
approach) will always object that the model says something about itself and the 
expectations of its creators, but not about the phenomenon being modeled.  But 
unless you can find some serious flaw in the way the model relates to the 
phenomenon, you are not justified in making such objections.  This is true of 
models in general, regardless of whether they are what I would call analytical or 
synthetic.

As for a "general theory of behavior," not only do I not believe that such a 
theory would be next-to impossible to develop, I don't even believe that the 
notion makes any sense.

> Have you ever read any Jungian stuff by Singer regarding transpersonal
> psychotherapy?

Nope.

-- Simon!


