Date: Fri, 12 Nov 93 12:20:05 PST
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Continuity!!!!...and other things!

> From: Bo <beau@metronet.org>

O.K., I was excessively terse, and will clarify.  Further discussion
should really be off-line.

> quote...    "...not very far..." Well DO they or DON'T they?? Nobody said
> to quantify it....because it's "not far" it doesn't count??? 

Electrons will travel some distance through air, of course -- nothing
attenuates anything perfectly.  A more relevant quantity is how far
they can travel through the glass face of your CRT.  I assure you
that when you're sitting on your couch, no great quantity of
electrons are escaping from your TV and ionizing you.  The fear
people had about CRTs was not that electrons would fail to stop, but
that they generate X-rays in stopping.

> I mentioned "broadcast power"...you called it just "electromagnetic
> radiation"...so's everything from gamma to microwaves...make your point!

My point was that EM radiation does not involve electrons, and is thus
irrelevant to the travel of electrons through air.

> And lightning...."current flow..." and how do you define current flow?

This is a little more subtle.  Let's say you have a coulomb of, ah,
isotopically-labeled electrons at point A.  You allow a current to
flow thence to point B by placing a copper wire between them.  Where
are the labeled electrons?  Not at B; they're over at the A end of
the wire.  My point here is that current flow need not involve the
ballistic travel of electrons (though that is a current).  In
lightning, current travels through air, but the electrons just do a
sort of bucket-brigade maneuver.  Fast electrons scatter in air and
become thermal electrons.

> ...photon bombardment, ionizing radiation...WHAT ever you label it.

CRTs are designed and intended to produce photon bombardment.  Beta
rays differ by more than a label.

